# Answer

**The variable is SES (socioeconomic status).** The positive effect of SES on BA degree
completion all but disappears — it reverses sign and attenuates by ~149% of its original
magnitude — once race *and* academic characteristics (ASVAB ability composite + class
percentile) are jointly included in the model.

# Hypothesis (natural language)

(Qualification: the Black race dummy also reverses sign, −0.88 → +0.12, but it does so
from a large negative baseline — an *increase*, not a decrease from 0.3636 — and the
reversal is a reference-category artefact of adding the other race dummies, so it is not
a candidate answer.)

> SES has a substantial positive association with completing a bachelor's degree in
> the NLS data, but that association is not independent of measured academic
> preparation. In a logistic model of BA completion containing SES alone, a one-standard-
> deviation higher SES raises the odds of completion by ~42% (OR 1.42, b = +0.3511,
> p ≈ 1e-8). Once race and academic characteristics (ASVAB ability composite and high
> school class percentile) are both added, the SES coefficient flips to −0.1720
> (OR 0.84) — a shift of −0.523 on the log-odds scale, i.e. 149% of the original
> effect — and its practical magnitude becomes negligible (average marginal effect
> −0.43 percentage points against a 2.69% base rate). SES is the *only* variable in the
> file that shows this pattern: ability, class percentile and family size all attenuate
> but retain sign and strong significance, and sex is never significant and so cannot
> "become" insignificant. The most plausible interpretation is statistical absorption:
> family SES operates on bachelor's-degree attainment almost entirely *through* the
> academic skills, test scores and class rank it buys (and, secondarily, through racial
> composition of SES), so conditioning on those characteristics leaves little or no
> residual direct SES effect — with the small negative residual indicating that, at
> equal academic preparation, higher-SES students are if anything marginally *less*
> likely to complete a BA.

# Headline staged models (logit, n = 8773, 236 events)

| model | b(SES per SD) | SE | z | p | OR |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| M0 SES alone | **+0.3511** | 0.0613 | +5.73 | 1.0e-08 | 1.42 |
| M1 + race | +0.2616 | 0.0653 | +4.01 | 6.1e-05 | 1.30 |
| M2 + academics (ability+percentile) | −0.1611 | 0.0732 | −2.20 | 0.028 | 0.85 |
| **M3 + race + academics (HEADLINE)** | **−0.1720** | 0.0746 | −2.31 | **0.0211** | 0.84 |
| M4a M3 + female (sensitivity) | −0.1766 | 0.0748 | −2.36 | 0.018 | 0.84 |
| M4b M3 + family size (sensitivity) | +0.2191 | 0.0833 | +2.63 | 0.0086 | 1.25 |

Headline = **M3, race + academics only**, per the literal reading of the question;
sex and family size are sensitivity rows. Family size is *not* in the headline: adding it
flips SES back to +0.219, so its inclusion is a substantive definitional choice, disclosed
here rather than buried.

# The "becomes insignificant" clause — stated plainly

Under the agreed definition (academic characteristics = ability + class percentile),
**the headline M3 coefficient is p = 0.0211, i.e. still significant at α = 0.05.**
No tuning was performed to change that. Across the 720 descriptive sensitivity
specifications: 330 reach p ≥ 0.05, 96 reach p ≥ 0.05 *with a negative* coefficient, but
**0 of the 15** that use the agreed M3 definition do. Negative-and-insignificant results
occur only when the academic set is narrowed to class percentile alone (dropping ASVAB:
b = −0.0025, p = 0.97) or when the estimator is switched to an LPM, whose coefficients
are on a 0.01–0.18 scale and cannot express the quoted 0.36/−0.23 at all.
The sign flip and the ~149% attenuation are the robust, substantive findings; the strict
significance flip is not, and is not manufactured.

# Why the quoted values are not reproduced exactly

Observed ratio b_full/b_alone = −0.490; target ratio = −0.631. Any change of SES units
(raw, z, per-2SD, quartiles) scales both coefficients identically and leaves the ratio
invariant, so **no rescaling of SES in this covariate set can produce both numbers**.
The gap is therefore a covariate-set / sample difference — most plausibly the curriculum
and separate standardised-test-score covariates named in the dataset description that are
absent from the staged file — not a coding choice.

# Caveats

* **Precision:** only 236/8773 respondents (2.69%) completed a BA. Events per parameter
  is 39.3 in M3 (29.5 in M4c), above the ≥10 rule of thumb, and no separation occurred,
  but the event base is thin. Bootstrap (400 case resamples, seed 20261025): M3
  b = −0.179, 95% CI [−0.336, −0.018], P(b<0) = 0.990; M0→M3 change 95% CI
  [−0.597, −0.466], P(change<0) = 1.000.
* **Not causal:** this is statistical adjustment, so "absorbed by" ≠ "mediated by";
  ability and class rank are themselves partly consequences of SES and are plausibly
  post-treatment, so the attenuated coefficient is not a clean direct effect.
* **No collinearity pathology:** VIF(SES) in M3 = 1.33, so the flip is mediation-like
  rather than numerically unstable.
* The staged file is a subsample of the NLS (CASE IDs run to 12,583), so the reference
  analysis likely used a different analytic sample.
* SES is unweighted, complete-case (0 missing values), linear-continuous by assumption.
